  "notes": "Hardener change: v2's card_settlement rule (v1's synthetic acquirer wording ACQUIRER SETTLEMENT / PAYGATE PAYOUT / CARD SETTLEMENT) was a bare regex with no word edges, so a Unicode letter glued to it (CAFÉACQUIRER SETTLEMENT) still counted as trading receipts. This is the same class as verify-32 finding 3. It is now a names list (same words, same rule id), with test_p_r4_v1_acquirer_wording_is_whole_words_in_any_script_too. v1 is untouched; v2's hash changes (no signed pack exists).\n\nGaps for the owner (not changed; each is a design or scope call):\n(1) The remaining regex-pattern rules in v2 do not fully follow design C1's 'whole words in any script'. invoice_transfer has a Unicode-aware leading \\b but no trailing edge, so 'INV 12É' or 'INV 123A' still matches; adding an edge would stop letter-suffixed invoice refs from matching, which is a behaviour call. loan_drawdown and loan_repayment ('\\bLOAN\\b|FINANCE\\b') have no leading edge on FINANCE, so 'REFINANCE LTD' is a loan drawdown. That is non-trading and can only lower M2. The debit-only patterns (hmrc_vat, hmrc_paye, owner_drawings, bank_fees, payroll) are v1 wording. A decision is needed on whether C1's whole-word rule covers pattern rules too.\n(2) No remote alerting, by design (local-first); the fail-closed table load stands in for an alert. No runbook entry beyond the README rollback line.\n(3) No changelog file exists in the repo, so no release-note line was written; the README is the user-facing note.\n(4) The judgement queue is R15/T21 per D60 and was deliberately not built here.\n(5) The changes are left uncommitted in the worktree for the pipeline to commit."
